  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 AM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nToday through Mon night...An upper level trough that moved \nthrough the UT- ID border region is gone, but a large closed low\nmoving through southern Canada will dominate this morning with \nnorthwesterly flow creating upslope conditions in the entire \nlength of the eastern highlands, from the MT border to the UT \nborder. As this low continues its progression eastward, \nprecipitation will end from south to north in this part of the \nforecast area. The western two thirds of the forecast area will \nexperience sunny to mostly sunny skies and dry conditions\ntoday. Dry conditions should continue for many locations, but\nthe next trough on the west coast will start pushing moisture\ninto central and eastern Idaho late tonight; mostly in the\ncentral Idaho mountains and again in the upper Snake River\nhighlands. Thus the upper Snake River highlands will be an area\nthat will see little let up in rain and snow, although it should\nbe less intense during the overnight hours. \n\nFor Sun and Sun night, it continues to be mostly a central \nIdaho mountains issue and upper Snake River highlands issue\nuntil late Sun night, when moisture spreads in everywhere. The\nupper level trough moving through generating this weather is\nslow to leave and the westerly flow in its wake continues the\nprecipitation through Mon, although by Mon night it finally\nends in the west and decreases in the east, even with an upper\nlevel moderate amplitude ridge sets up with its axis to the west\nof the forecast area. \n\nTemperatures stay cool in the northwest flow with little warming\ndue to the cold air advection with only clearer skies to aid\nthings. More warming develops for Sun with a shift to southerly\nand southwesterly flow, in spite of increasing cloud cover and\nprecipitation in the north. Temperatures should be mild\novernight on Sun night with all the cloud cover. \n\nBreezy wind limited to the Snake River plain and eastern Magic\nValley today, but the upcoming lows do not come with a lot of\nupper level support and wind will not be a major factor with\nthese storms.",
